                    "type": "string",
                    "description": "Next-page URL. Empty when there are no more pages."
                  }
                },
                "description": "Tool-specific result payload; see the inner properties for fields.",
                "required": [
                  "rhs_account_number",
                  "results"
                ],
                "additionalProperties": false
              },
              "guide": {
                "type": "string",
                "description": "For the headline answer to \"is this order still working?\", use state_group ('open' vs 'closed'); raw state has finer granularity if the user asks why. For the fill price the user cares about, use average_price (VWAP across fills). cumulative_quantity vs quantity tells \"how much filled?\"; canceled_quantity only appears on partial-fill-then-cancel paths. Asset symbol is currency_code; pair UUID is currency_pair_id. For current market, call get_crypto_quotes with the pair symbol (construct as currency_code + \"-USD\" for USD-quoted pairs, or look up via get_currency_pairs) — get_crypto_quotes takes the symbol, NOT currency_pair_id. initiator_type tells how the order was placed (app / API / recurring / agent) — surface it when the agent didn't place this order. Per-fill executions[] are omitted from list responses to keep the page sized for an agent's context window; pass order_id to retrieve a single order with executions populated. Present currency_code, side, type (ALWAYS translated to the app's name: stop_loss = \"stop order\", stop_limit = \"stop limit order\" — never show the raw stop_loss/stop_limit values to the user), state, quantity (or cumulative_quantity when partially filled), average_price once filled, backup withholding on a sell whenever tax_withholding is present, created_at, and id when cancellation may follow; suppress other fields unless asked. When presenting multiple orders, add a Fee column when any order on the page carries fee or fee_rate — the fee value verbatim (label it estimated when the order has no fills yet), with the fee_rate value appended verbatim in parentheses when present, e.g. '$1.23 (0.05% maker)' or '$1.23 (0.95%)' — fee_rate carries its own maker/taker/blended qualifier, never re-derive or re-label it; an order with fee_rate but no fee shows the bare rate; '-' for orders with neither; omit the column entirely when no order on the page has either. For a single order, state the fee and rate the same way when present. Backup withholding: show whichever of tax_withholding.estimated_withholding_amount / total_withholding_amount is present (exactly one ever is), labeled \"Estimated backup withholding\" for the estimate and \"Backup withholding\" for the total, with the tax_withholding.withholding_rate value appended verbatim in parentheses, e.g. '$23.88 (24%)'; on a partially filled order phrase the total as withheld so far — never as final, and never compute an estimate for the remainder. When presenting multiple orders, add a Withholding column only when at least one order on the page carries tax_withholding, with '-' for orders that do not; when none does, do not mention withholding at all. Answer total cost/credit questions from whichever net_* field the order carries, never from rounded_executed_notional / rounded_estimated_notional_with_estimated_fee / total_executed_notional — those are gross of taxes like backup withholding and will overstate a subject order. An order with no tax_withholding has no withholding — never raise the subject for it or show it as $0. Every order on this page belongs to the account echoed in rhs_account_number / crypto_account_number — when querying several accounts in parallel, attribute each order from its own response's echo, never from memory; identify the account to the user by crypto_account_number."
              }
